WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.500
A circuit formed without one protein.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.920
Switch the protein on in an adult.

3
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.820
Can that undo the damage?

4
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.810
Rett syndrome puts this to the test.

5
00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.370
Girls with it seem to develop typically for many months.

6
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:19.573
Then they lose purposeful hand use and speech.

7
00:00:20.617 --> 00:00:25.677
Most cases come from mutations in one gene, MECP2, on the X chromosome.

8
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:31.727
Its protein binds methylated DNA, and is thought to help set the level of many

9
00:00:31.727 --> 00:00:32.727
genes.

10
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.010
Mice lacking the gene appear typical for several weeks.

11
00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.210
Then they develop tremor, breathing changes and reduced movement.

12
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:47.183
Their neurons do not die in large numbers.

13
00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:50.043
They are smaller, with simpler dendrites.

14
00:00:52.917 --> 00:00:58.417
In 2007, Jacky Guy, Adrian Bird and colleagues built mice in which the gene is

15
00:00:58.417 --> 00:00:59.417
silent.

16
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:01.787
A blocking sequence silences it.

17
00:01:02.527 --> 00:01:04.367
The mice also carry an enzyme, Cre.

18
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.480
Cre enters the nucleus only after the drug tamoxifen is given.

19
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.400
Then it cuts out the block.

20
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:20.750
Predict. If the circuit was built wrong for good, what does restoring the protein in

21
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.430
a sick adult do? And if it only needs the protein to run?

22
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.120
Many symptoms reversed, in young adult mice and in older adult mice.

23
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:40.193
In typical mice, tamoxifen did nothing.

24
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:47.193
So circuits that formed without the protein can work again once it returns.

25
00:01:48.053 --> 00:01:50.153
That does not show their development was normal.

26
00:01:51.183 --> 00:01:52.543
Now the other test.

27
00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:58.063
In 2011, Huda Zoghbi's laboratory deleted the gene in adult mice.

28
00:01:59.117 --> 00:02:02.597
They developed symptoms like those of mice that never had it.

29
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:05.057
The protein is needed continuously.

30
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.430
Both late tests point the same way.

31
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.050
The adult circuit still needs this protein.

32
00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.550
A building role is not ruled out.

33
00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:19.343
The balance of excitation and inhibition shifts too, and in one brain it runs both

34
00:02:19.343 --> 00:02:22.363
ways. The forebrain tends to be underactive.

35
00:02:23.223 --> 00:02:25.043
The brainstem tilts toward excitation.

36
00:02:27.517 --> 00:02:30.497
One limit. This is a mouse result.

37
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.737
Whether it holds for an older person is not known.

38
00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.270
A second limit. The dose matters.

39
00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.450
Too much of this protein causes a separate syndrome in males.

40
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:49.190
Takeaway: some traits can still be reversed, and some proteins are still needed in

41
00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.970
adults. The late tests ask both questions.
